 "md": {
  "rejected": "## `rj-audit-inbox-edges-silently-skipped`\nAn edge written in an inbox file is not an edge in the memory until someone has checked. The merger skips an edge whose relation it does not know, or whose end is missing, and says so only on the console; the file is then moved to `merged/` and the check passes. By 2026-11-09, 229 edges had been lost this way. The cost is concrete: a decision that was replaced in words (the 3D teacher, the studio evidence rule, the old benches) still comes back from a query as current. What to do instead: use only the seven relations, and make the merger fail rather than skip.\n",
  "measurements": "## `ms-audit-inbox-edges-dropped-2026-11-09`\nWhole-project audit, docs and memory. 229 of 3,062 edges in merged inbox files are not in the graph. The 96 that are well-formed are carried again by this file; the 130 with a relation the graph does not have need a person to map them (`informs` 51, `implements` 13, `refines` 10, `supports` 10 are the common ones).\n\n## `ms-audit-kit-census-2026-11-09`\n830 topics, 12,597 items, 38 files under `data/kits/`. Replaces the 2026-10-02 census (565 / 7,385), which older decisions still cite. The verified share was not recounted.\n\n## `ms-audit-open-items-closed-2026-11-09`\nTen items listed as open in `open.md` and `architecture.md` are done in the code. Each is listed with the line that closes it in `docs/design/round7/audit/docs-memory.md` (DOCS-22 and DOCS-23). Others in those notes are only partly closed and are left open on purpose.\n",
  "decisions": "## `dc-audit-india-move-not-cut-over`\nThe memory read as if production had moved to India. It has not: the move was decided on 2026-10-04 and never cut over. This entry records the state so that a query stops returning the move as current; it decides nothing new.\n\n## `dc-audit-prod-db-neon-us-east-1`\nThe first infrastructure entry names a Singapore database and Vercel functions. Neither is where production runs. This entry records the state; it decides nothing new.\n"
